zker secures the Democratic nomination, or the national political environment swings sharply, Illinois's margin could move several points in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Illinois remains a Democratic-leaning state in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of about six points toward Democrats. The race is open, with a term-limited Republican president leaving the ballot without an incumbent, so neither side carries the usual advantages of White House incumbency. No public polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available for this race, leaving the state's partisan lean to do most of the work.\n\nThe main state-specific storyline is Governor JB Pritzker's active pursuit of the 2028 Democratic nomination, including a $20 million group to aid midterm Democrats. A home-state nominee could add a point or two to the Democratic margin, though his candidacy is far from assured. The forecast therefore sits close to Illinois's underlying lean, with wide uncertainty reflecting the absence of polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7608, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.746, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7069, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7035, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.7139, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7208, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7158, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.714, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.7198, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.727, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.7103, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.733, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.709